

Dictionary Methods in Python



📚 Concepts Covered

keys()

values()

items()

get()

update()

pop()

popitem()

clear()


💻 Practice Code

1️⃣ keys() – Get All Keys

student = {
    "name": "Veshma",
    "age": 21,
    "course": "Python"
}

print(student.keys())

Output

dict_keys(['name', 'age', 'course'])


---

2️⃣ values() – Get All Values

student = {
    "name": "Veshma",
    "age": 21,
    "course": "Python"
}

print(student.values())

Output

dict_values(['Veshma', 21, 'Python'])


---

3️⃣ items() – Get Key-Value Pairs

student = {
    "name": "Veshma",
    "age": 21
}

print(student.items())

Output

dict_items([('name', 'Veshma'), ('age', 21)])


---

4️⃣ get() – Access Values Safely

student = {
    "name": "Veshma",
    "course": "Python"
}

print(student.get("name"))
print(student.get("salary", "Not Found"))

Output

Veshma
Not Found


---

5️⃣ update() – Add or Modify Data

student = {
    "name": "Veshma",
    "course": "Python"
}

student.update({"course": "Data Analytics"})

print(student)

Output

{'name': 'Veshma', 'course': 'Data Analytics'}


---

6️⃣ pop() and popitem()

student = {
    "name": "Veshma",
    "age": 21,
    "course": "Python"
}

student.pop("age")
print(student)

student.popitem()
print(student)

Output

{'name': 'Veshma', 'course': 'Python'}
{'name': 'Veshma'}


---

7️⃣ clear() – Remove All Items

student = {
    "name": "Veshma",
    "course": "Python"
}

student.clear()

print(student)

Output

{}

🌍 Real-World Example

employee = {
    "id": 101,
    "name": "Rahul",
    "salary": 50000
}

for key, value in employee.items():
    print(key, ":", value)

Output

id : 101
name : Rahul
salary : 50000



keys() → Returns all dictionary keys.

values() → Returns all dictionary values.

items() → Returns key-value pairs.

get() → Access values without errors.

update() → Add or modify dictionary data.

pop() → Removes a specific key.

popitem() → Removes the last inserted item.

clear() → Deletes all items.


🚀 Mini Practice

Count Word Frequency

text = "python python sql"

words = text.split()

frequency = {}

for word in words:
    frequency[word] = frequency.get(word, 0) + 1

print(frequency)

Output

{'python': 2, 'sql': 1}

